1. Set up Linux environment

Installed WSL2 on Windows to run a Linux kernel.

Installed Ubuntu from the Microsoft Store as the Linux distribution.

Confirmed Ubuntu was running (wsl -l -v).

2. Installed Docker

Uninstalled conflicting packages (if any).

Set up Docker using Docker’s official apt repository in Ubuntu.

Started the Docker service and verified it with:

sudo docker ps


Ensured USE_GPU=off if you don’t have a GPU.

3. Cloned the VC Trainer & Player repository

Initially cloned a stable version (ver_1.1) but later switched to main branch for latest updates:

git clone https://github.com/w-okada/voice-changer.git
cd voice-changer


Removed old version if it existed (rm -rf voice-changer) to avoid conflicts.

4. Selected models

Needed matching model files: .pth (weights) + .json (config) — same base name.

Optional: .onnx for ONNX framework.

Set the framework (PyTorch) and device (CPU if no GPU) in the GUI.

5. Checked audio setup

Tried arecord -l to detect input devices, but WSL2 doesn’t natively pass through Windows microphones, so live input didn’t work.

Alternatives:

Use Windows Docker to access mic directly.

Feed pre-recorded WAV files instead of live input.

6. Ran VC Trainer & Player

Started the server via:

USE_GPU=off bash start2.sh MMVC


Verified server was running and Docker containers were active (docker ps).

Encountered errors when .json config was missing (AttributeError: 'hps') → fixed by selecting the correct config file.

7. Observed limitations

Without GPU: slower conversion (CPU mode).

Without proper audio passthrough in WSL2: vol(rms) stayed 0 → live mic input didn’t work.

VC conversion itself works only if model + config are properly loaded and input audio is available.

So basically the pipeline was:

Windows → WSL2 → Ubuntu

Ubuntu → Docker installed

Docker → VC Trainer & Player container runs

Load model (.pth) + config (.json)

Feed audio (live or pre-recorded)

Converted audio outputs (real-time if mic works, otherwise from file)
